<p>When a supplier puts its prices up, or cement goes up across the market, you can change hundreds of prices in one go instead of editing
  each product. Owners, managers and anyone allowed to manage the catalogue can do it.</p>

<h2>Changing prices by a rule</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Open <strong>Stock › Products</strong>, press the <strong>⚙</strong> menu and choose <strong>Change prices</strong>.</li>
  <li>Under <strong>Which products</strong>, choose <strong>All products</strong>, a <strong>category</strong> (its subcategories are
    included), a <strong>brand</strong>, or a <strong>supplier's products</strong>.</li>
  <li>Choose whether to change by a <strong>percentage</strong> or an <strong>amount</strong> in shillings, and type it. Use a minus sign to
    lower prices: <code>-5</code> takes 5% off.</li>
  <li>Choose whether to <strong>round new prices</strong> to the nearest 100, 500, 1,000 or 5,000 shillings, so the shelf prices stay tidy.</li>
  <li>Tick <strong>Packs with their own price too</strong> to change the price of a bag, box or bundle as well, and <strong>Contractor and
    wholesale prices too</strong> to change price lists as well.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Preview</strong>. HardPoint lists every price that will change, old and new, and changes nothing yet.</li>
  <li>If it looks right, press <strong>Apply</strong> and confirm.</li>
</ol>
<p>The change is recorded in the activity log, with who made it and what it was. Sales already made keep the price they were sold at, and
  margins in reports use the cost on the day each sale was made.</p>

<h2>Examples</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Your cement supplier puts prices up 8%: <strong>Supplier's products</strong>, that supplier, percentage, <code>8</code>, round to the
    nearest 100.</li>
  <li>Clearing a brand: the brand, percentage, <code>-15</code>, without contractor prices.</li>
  <li>Transport costs have gone up on everything heavy: the Building materials category, amount, <code>50</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Changes that don't follow a rule</h2>
<p>When each product needs its own new price, use a spreadsheet instead: press <strong>Export CSV</strong> on the Products list, change the
  price column and import the file again. See <%= link_to "Importing products from a spreadsheet", help_article_path("importing-products"), class: "link" %>.</p>

<h2>After changing prices</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Reprint shelf labels for the changed products with <strong>Print labels</strong> in the Products ⚙ menu.</li>
  <li>Tills pick up the new prices on the next sale. The offline till picks them up the next time it's opened online.</li>
  <li>Quotes already sent keep their prices until they expire.</li>
</ul>
